## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design real-time data quality and drift detection at scale, balancing schema evolution, validation, quarantine, alerting, and cross-account governance using AWS services.

## Key Concepts
- Schema evolution and registry management
- Real-time validation and data quarantining
- Observability: CloudWatch metrics and SNS alerts
- Cross-account auditability via Glue Data Catalog and Lake Formation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo validator for a streaming record
function isRecordValid(r){
  if(!r.user_id || !r.timestamp) return false;
  if(typeof r.value !== 'number' || r.value < 0) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift detection across regions and accounts?
- What are trade-offs of using a separate quarantine bucket vs inline rejection for analytics workloads?

## Why This Is Asked

Rationale: tests end-to-end ingestion, schema evolution in a multi-tenant IoT lake, data quality, and handling late data with canaries.

## Key Concepts

- Glue Schema Registry and Avro evolution
- Nested Avro schemas and optional fields
- Kinesis Data Streams + Glue ETL (Spark) + Parquet in S3
- Glue Data Catalog and partitioning by day
- Data quality checks, dedup, and late-arrival handling
- Canary testing and cost/latency trade-offs between Glue vs EMR Spark

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: evolution-safe defaults for missing fields
function applyDefaults(record, schema) {
  const out = {};
  for (const f of schema.fields) {
    if (record.hasOwnProperty(f.name)) out[f.name] = record[f.name];
    else if (f.default !== undefined) out[f.name] = f.default;
    else out[f.name] = null;
  }
  return out;
}
```

